{"requirements":[{"id":"tmp_hardening","requirement":"Mount /tmp with nosuid, nodev, and noexec, with settings surviving reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before the change /tmp was not a distinct mount. After configuration it was a tmpfs with nosuid,nodev,noexec; execution and device-node access probes failed as intended. Following an observed reboot, the mount and options remained active with mode 1777.","evidence_ids":["cmd-47dcf2625c58462d","cmd-75f113b9166b436e","cmd-82c1da87f3b74bf5","cmd-e61c5676d1b6475d","cmd-079ea9e428d047ac","cmd-f3c4379c7f0d40f6","cmd-10d0d15ab0ca4977","cmd-3e28b71a68e74922"]},{"id":"dev_shm_hardening","requirement":"Mount /dev/shm with nosuid, nodev, and noexec, with settings surviving reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original /dev/shm mount lacked noexec. The final mount had nosuid,nodev,noexec; execution and device-node access probes failed as intended. The same options and mode 1777 were directly observed after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-47dcf2625c58462d","cmd-75f113b9166b436e","cmd-82c1da87f3b74bf5","cmd-e61c5676d1b6475d","cmd-079ea9e428d047ac","cmd-f3c4379c7f0d40f6","cmd-10d0d15ab0ca4977","cmd-3e28b71a68e74922"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.86,"summary":"System health remained good: no failed services or package damage appeared, and network exposure was unchanged apart from the expected post-reboot SSH PID change. Two low-risk hygiene issues are visible. cmd-47dcf2625c58462d created baseline marker files that were never used in a post-restart data comparison and were subsequently removed by the new volatile mounts/reboot. cmd-75f113b9166b436e created auxiliary /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening and /etc/fstab.mount-hardening files; the former had a reasonable rollback role, but neither has captured cleanup, leaving the staging file—and possibly the backup—as retained residue. Required probe files and device nodes were explicitly removed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-47dcf2625c58462d","cmd-75f113b9166b436e","cmd-82c1da87f3b74bf5","cmd-3e28b71a68e74922","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both requested filesystems were hardened successfully, and a bounded reboot directly demonstrated persistence and recovery. The restart protocol showed a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered system, local-filesystem, and mount state; the fixed lifecycle boot observations independently agree.","limitations":["The setuid effect was established by directly observing the nosuid mount option, but it was not behaviorally isolated with an executable setuid binary; the script probes were also blocked by noexec.","The restart evidence did not perform a meaningful before/after data comparison: its marker files were placed on filesystems intentionally configured as volatile tmpfs. This does not undermine the requested mount persistence but slightly limits restart-protocol completeness.","No final inventory of /etc confirms whether the backup and staging files created by cmd-75f113b9166b436e remained; the timeline contains no command removing them."]}
